=== QT001 | a literal dictionary whose sides differ in length
f:{[] `a`b!1 2 3}

=== clean | a literal dictionary that balances
f:{[] `a`b!1 2}

=== clean | keys and values built at runtime are not a literal
f:{[k;v] k!v}

=== QT002 | a symbol passed to a function that is visibly numeric
f:{[r] r+1};f[`bad]

=== clean | the same function given a number
f:{[r] r+1};f[2]

=== QT003 | a symbol under arithmetic is a runtime type error
2+`a

=== QT003 | either side, and vectors likewise
1 2+`a`b

=== clean | chars are numeric citizens; none of these error
r1:1.5+"a"
r2:"a"*3
r3:2-"x"

=== clean | take builds symbol vectors without arithmetic
2#`a`b`c

=== QT001 | the value scan stops at the end of the line, as q does
d:`a`b!1 2 3

=== clean | the same dictionary balanced, with no semicolon after it
d:`a`b!1 2

=== QT004 | a symbol-named cast on a string converts char codes
n:`long$"123"

=== QT004 | the date cast is ten dates, not one
d:`date$"2024.01.01"

=== QT004 | symbol by name is a type error
s:`symbol$"abc"

=== clean | the uppercase-char cast parses text
n:"J"$"123"

=== clean | the bare-backtick cast makes a symbol
s:`$"abc"

=== clean | char and byte are legitimate on a string
c:`char$"ab";b:`byte$"ab"

=== clean | a cast on a non-string
n:`long$1.5

=== QT005 | every column scalar is a rank error
t:([] a:1; b:`x)

=== QT005 | keyed too
t:([k:1] v:2)

=== clean | one vector column extends the rest
t:([] a:1; b:2 3)

=== clean | enlist makes the row
t:([] a:enlist 1)

=== clean | a string column is a vector
t:([] a:"ab")

=== clean | an empty column
t:([] a:())

=== QT006 | mismatched literal vectors under plus
r:1 2 3+4 5

=== QT006 | mismatched symbol vectors under equals
r:`a`b=`a`b`c

=== QT006 | minus with spaces on both sides is the operator
r:1 2 3 - 1 2

=== clean | minus with a space before and none after is a negative literal
r:1 2 3 -1 2

=== clean | a negative exponent is part of the float, not an infix minus
r:0.01 0.558313 9e-08 0.3 0.4 0.5

=== clean | nor is a positive one
r:1.5e+10 2.5e-3 3e4

=== clean | scientific notation either side of an infix, at equal lengths
r:1e-3 2e-3=3e-3 4e-3

=== QT006 | a mismatch is still a mismatch when an operand uses an exponent
r:1 2 9e-08+4 5

=== clean | same length
r:1 2 3+4 5 6

=== clean | a scalar extends
r:1 2 3+4

=== clean | join takes any lengths
r:1 2,3 4 5

=== QT007 | ssr on a symbol
r:ssr[`abc;"a";"b"]

=== QT007 | a symbol as the pattern
r:ssr["abc";`a;"b"]

=== QT007 | infix ss on a symbol
r:`abc ss "a"

=== clean | ssr on a string
r:ssr["abc";"a";"b"]

=== clean | trim and lower accept symbols
r:trim `a;s:lower `A

=== clean | a cast to string is not a symbol literal
r:ss[`char$x;"a"]

=== QA011 | a boolean vector cannot be a conditional's test
r:$[101b;1;2]

=== QA011 | nor a numeric vector
r:$[1 2 3;1;2]

=== QA011 | nor a symbol
r:$[`a;1;2]

=== clean | an atom is what $ wants
r:$[1b;1;2]

=== clean | and a long atom counts
r:$[1;1;2]

=== QT015 QB018 | a symbol against a number is a type error, and both sides are literals
r:1=`a

=== QT015 | and against a string
r:"abc"=`abc

=== QT015 QB018 | ordering comparisons too, and again both are literals
r:`x<1

=== QB018 | symbol against symbol is not a type error, but it is still two literals
r:`a=`b

=== clean | a char compares by its code, which q allows
r:1="a"

=== clean | match never raises, whatever the types
r:1~`a

=== clean | a symbol naming a cast is not an operand
r:$[0i=`int$period;1;2]

=== clean | 0! unkeys a table; the 0 is not the operand
r:`time in 0!t

=== QT008 | til wants an integer
r:til[1.5]

=== clean | an integer is what it wants
r:til[5]

=== QT009 | where wants booleans or counts
r:where[1.5 2.5]

=== clean | booleans are the usual argument
r:where[101b]

=== QT010 | a numeric aggregate over symbols
r:sum[`a`b]

=== clean | the same aggregate over numbers
r:sum[1 2 3]

=== QT011 | sorting an atom sorts nothing
r:asc[1]

=== clean | a list is what asc is for
r:asc[3 1 2]

=== QT012 | distinct of an atom likewise
r:distinct[1]

=== clean | a list again
r:distinct[1 1 2]

=== QT013 | flip wants something with two dimensions
r:flip[1 2]

=== clean | a dictionary of lists has them
r:flip[`a`b!(1 2;3 4)]

=== QT014 | a rotate count must be an integer atom
r:rotate[1.5;1 2]

=== clean | an integer count
r:rotate[1;1 2 3]

=== QT020 | vector columns of different lengths
t:([]a:1 2;b:3 4 5)

=== QT020 | enlist makes a one-item vector, which does not extend
t:([]a:enlist 1;b:2 3)

=== QT020 | on the key side too
t:([k:1 2]v:3 4 5)

=== clean | an atom beside a vector extends to its length
t:([]a:1;b:2 3)

=== clean | equal lengths
t:([]a:1 2;b:3 4)

=== clean | a boolean vector is a vector: two bits, two rows
t:([]a:10b;b:1)

=== clean | a byte vector likewise
t:([]a:0x0102;b:1)

=== QT005 | one bit is a boolean atom, so every column here is an atom
t:([]a:1b;b:1)

=== QT005 | a keyed table needs vectors on both sides
t:([k:1]v:2 3)

=== QT005 | a single char is an atom, whatever the quotes suggest
t:([]a:"a";b:1)

=== clean | a two-char string is a two-row column
t:([]a:"ab";b:1)

=== QT021 | ss with an empty pattern
r:ss["abc";""]

=== QT021 | ssr likewise
r:ssr["abc";"";"b"]

=== clean | a pattern with something in it
r:ss["abc";"a"]

=== QT008 | til with a float, written by juxtaposition - the spelling real q uses
r:til 1.5

=== QT010 | a numeric aggregate over symbols, juxtaposed
r:sum `a`b

=== QT011 | sorting an atom, juxtaposed
r:asc 1

=== clean | a name as the argument says nothing about its type
r:asc x

=== clean | the literal is not the whole argument: q reads right to left, so this is til of a product
r:til 1.5*x

=== clean | nor here, where the vector is joined to something before sum sees it
r:sum 1 2 3,x

=== QT006 | a byte vector has one item per pair of hex digits
r:1 2 3+0x0102

=== QT006 | a parenthesised list has one item per top-level semicolon plus one
r:(1;2;3)+4 5

=== clean | the same shapes at matching lengths
r:1 2+0x0102

=== QT001 | a dictionary's values as a parenthesised list, one short
d:`a`b!(1;2;3)

=== QT015 | a number tested for membership in symbols
r:1 in `a`b

=== QT015 | and a symbol in numbers
r:`a in 1 2

=== clean | symbol membership in symbols is what in is for
r:`a in `a`b
